      "writtenOn": "2025-11-11T05:48:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just realized that, for v6.18, below code is introduced to indicate the PV interfaces which should use inout mechanism.\n\n```\n\tPKVM_FIRST_INOUT_PV_INTERFACE,\n\t__pkvm__vm_destroy \u003d PKVM_FIRST_INOUT_PV_INTERFACE,\n```\n\nThe `PKVM_FIRST_INOUT_PV_INTERFACE` is a valid PV interface number as `__pkvm_vm_destroy` is set to `PKVM_FIRST_INOUT_PV_INTERFACE`.\n\nWith including `kvm_pkvm_hypercalls.h`, probably also can add one more line, e.g., as PKVM_HC(inout), and put all the PV interfaces which use inout mechanism below this line. Then PKVM_HC(inout) is a placeholder and won\u0027t be used as a PV interface by the host, meanwhile there is also no handler in the pKVM for it. So it will not be a valid PV interface number any more.",
      "revId": "19e533e94040afc2959502b4b8b28d10ab6705dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dac654af_f23bcd1c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_pkvm_hypercalls.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-11T15:15:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I imagine we could address this a different way: instead of having this PKVM_FIRST_INOUT_PV_INTERFACE as a \"delimiter\", use a separate macro `PKVM_HC_INOUT()` for those interfaces that should use inout, and add something like this to (kvm_)pkvm.h:\n\n```\nstatic inline bool pkvm_fn_use_inout(enum pkvm_fn fn)\n{\n\tswitch (fn) {\n\t#define PKVM_HC(fn)\n\t#define PKVM_HC_INOUT(fn)\tcase fn:\n\t#include \u003casm/kvm_pkvm_hypercalls.h\u003e\n\t\treturn true;\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n...BTW maybe also worth renaming `pkvm_fn` to `pkvm_hc`?",
      "parentUuid": "b7362be8_9ef5a65f",
      "revId": "19e533e94040afc2959502b4b8b28d10ab6705dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51cecc10_3006f4cf",
        "filename": "arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_pkvm_hypercalls.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-12T00:57:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That sounds attractive. With introducing additional macro PKVM_HC_INOUT, it should be defined at the other places where use it, i.e., `enum pkvm_fn` and `char *pkvm_hypercalls`. But seems these two places doesn\u0027t need to treat PKVM_HC_INOUT special, so probably we can add below in `kvm_pkvm_hypercalls.h`, to define PKVM_HC_INOUT as PKVM_HC if it is not defined.\n```\n#ifndef PKVM_HC_INOUT\n#define PKVM_HC_INOUT PKVM_HC\n#endif\n```\n\nAnd probably also undefine PKVM_HC_INOUT in the end.\n\n\u003e...BTW maybe also worth renaming pkvm_fn to pkvm_hc?\n\nTo me, `pkvm_fn` represents a function in the pKVM, and the name of `kvm_call_pkvm` explicitly means kvm call a function in the pKVM. This implicitly means kvm executes pKVM\u0027s hypercalls. With renaming `kvm_call_pkvm` to `pkvm_hypercall`, that implicit meaning becomes explicitly. In this case, probably renaming `pkvm_fn` to `pkvm_hc` is better.\n\nAnd the hander in the pKVM side is called pkvm_handle_kvm_call(), which is to reflect the macro `kvm_call_pkvm`. With the new name `pkvm_hypercall`, maybe also rename this handler to `pkvm_handle_host_hypercalls()`?",
      "parentUuid": "dac654af_f23bcd1c",
      "revId": "19e533e94040afc2959502b4b8b28d10ab6705dd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
